WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
- The dollar is getting stronger

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.900
as it's starting to look like the US

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.970
has the strongest economy in the world,

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.560
but that's probably bad news

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.720
for US companies and the stock market.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.700
I'm going to explain
what's happening in markets

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.770
that's pushing the value
of the dollar higher,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.560
why it's happening now and
is expected to continue,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.530
and why a stronger dollar
typically means tough times

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:20.790
for US stocks.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:21.720
Let's get into it.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.830
After rising to a 20-year
high in September 2022,

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.120
the dollar started falling

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.060
and largely kept falling
until July of this year.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.980
The currency weakened pretty consistently,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.980
declining along with inflation
as the stock market rose,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.630
especially tech stocks.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.250
But all that changed in July,

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.680
as US government bond
yields started to rise,

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.990
and stock prices started to fall.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.190
Higher government bond yields

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.350
make currencies more attractive to traders

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.330
because they need a
country's local currency

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.680
to buy its government bonds.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.450
When traders think a country's bond yields

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:52.650
are going to keep rising,

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.480
they typically buy up its currency,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.250
and that pushes up the value.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.290
This is an especially lucrative strategy

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.020
for traders who buy when
a currency's value is low,

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.160
and it appreciates.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.740
Not only are they rewarded
by the high bond yields

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.330
but the currency's higher value

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.980
gives them an additional return.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.900
Currencies also tend to rise in value

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.550
when a country's economy strengthens

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.410
in comparison to other countries.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.670
That's what's happened to the US

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.080
over the past couple months.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.820
July was when China's economy

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.620
first started showing signs of stress.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:23.100
Weak retail sales, a big slowdown
in its real estate market,

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.580
and rising unemployment have
all slowed down Chinese growth.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.220
That has had a negative
impact on many Asian

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.540
and European countries

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.710
because China's appetite

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.500
for their goods was helping
fuel their economies.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.390
As Chinese demand pulled back,

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.060
economic growth in countries
that used euros, pounds,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.600
and dollars, other than
the US, started to weaken.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.690
The US economy is mostly
supported by spending on services,

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.720
like doctors, lawyers, and Uber drivers.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.020
So a slowdown in global trade
doesn't hurt quite as much

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.290
as it does in places
like Germany or Vietnam.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.540
Win Thin, Global Head of Currency Strategy

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:58.590
at Brown Brothers Harriman,

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:01.470
recently said, "If we look
everywhere around the world,

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.930
the US is sort of like
the last man standing,

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.270
and the dollar is coming
strong this year."

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.590
With a stronger economy,

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.290
the central bank of the
US, the Federal Reserve,

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:11.880
is expected to hold rates higher

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.800
for longer than other central banks.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.470
Unfortunately, this is
not typically a good thing

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.700
for the stock market.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.300
When the dollar hit its highest
level since 2002 last year,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.630
half of all S&amp;P 500 companies
mentioned the strong dollar

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:25.980
in their earnings reports,

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.020
and most of them weren't celebrating.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.320
The S&amp;P had its worst year since 2008.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.410
Certainly, that wasn't all
about the strong dollar,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.090
but as lots of CEOs told us

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.280
on their earnings calls, it didn't help.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.440
A strong dollar makes items more expensive

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:41.460
in other countries,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.280
so American companies
often sell fewer goods

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.290
when the dollar's value rises.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.430
They also make less money

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.110
because when they convert
their foreign sales

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.930
back to dollars, they
often wind up with less.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.090
In Q2, Apple saw its revenue decline

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.800
for the third quarter in a row

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.020
and said that if it weren't
for the strong dollar,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.430
its revenue would've gone up.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:01.110
With the dollar strengthening again,

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.510
look for more and more
companies to talk about it

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.010
in the upcoming earnings season.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:05.970
It could also lead

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.070
to more companies reporting lower earnings

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.030
in the third quarter,

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.250
and with the economy starting to slow down

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.500
and fears about a recession rising,

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:15.091
that could really dent the stock market.

00:03:15.091 --> 00:03:18.258
(bright music fades)

